===Etymology 1===
{{etymon|en|:inh|enm:felen<ety:inh<ang:fēlan>>|tree=1}}
From {{inh|en|enm|felen}}, from {{inh|en|ang|fēlan}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*fōlijan}}.<ref name="EDPG">{{R:gem:EDPG|*fōljan-|150}}</ref>

Cognate with {{cog|nl|voelen}} and {{cog|de|fühlen}}.

====Verb====
{{wikidata lexeme|L188}}
{{en-verb|~s|~ing|felt}}

# {{lb|en|heading}} ''To use or experience the sense of touch.''
## {{lb|en|transitive|copulative}} To become aware of through the [[skin]]; to use the [[sense]] of [[touch]] on.
##: {{ux|en|You can '''feel''' a heartbeat if you put your fingers on your breast.}}
##: {{ux|en|I '''felt''' cold and miserable all night.}}
##: {{ux|en|I did '''feel''' a complete fool after stumbling on the same rock again.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|author=Joshua Kosman|quotee=AV Vilavong|title=Deaf interpreter helps the emotion of music come through|journal=w:San Francisco Chronicle|url=https://www.sfchronicle.com/entertainment/music/article/deaf-interpreter-helps-the-emotion-of-music-come-21173962.php|date=7 December 2022|passage=Every deaf person has different levels of hearing that enable them to '''feel''' the beats and the vibrations of the music.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[find]] one's [[way]] ([[literally]] or [[figuratively]]) by touching or using cautious movements.
##: {{ux|en|I '''felt''' my way through the darkened room.}}
##: {{ux|en|I '''felt''' my way cautiously through the dangerous business maneuver.}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[receive]] [[information]] by [[touch]] or by any [[neuron]]s other than those responsible for sight, smell, taste, or hearing.
## {{lb|en|intransitive}} To search by sense of touch.
##: {{ux|en|He '''felt''' (about) for the light switch in the dark.}}
# {{lb|en|heading}} ''To sense or think emotionally or judgmentally.''
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To experience an emotion or other mental state about.
##: {{ux|en|I can '''feel''' the sadness in his poems.}}
##* {{RQ:Pope Universal Prayer|stanza=X|page=6|passage=Teach me to '''feel''' another's VVoe; / To hide the Fault I ſee; / That Mercy I to others ſhovv, / That Mercy ſhow to me.}}
##* {{RQ:Hough Purchase Price|chapter=I|passage=Captain Edward Carlisle, soldier as he was, martinet as he was, '''felt''' a curious sensation of helplessness seize upon him as he met her steady gaze, her alluring smile ; he could not tell what this prisoner might do.}}
##* {{RQ:Economist|date=2013-08-10|volume=408|issue=8848|author=Lexington|title=Keeping the mighty honest|url=http://www.economist.com/news/united-states/21583274-new-wave-press-barons-should-not-allow-newspapers-become-niche-products-keeping|passage=British journalists shun complete respectability, '''feeling''' a duty to be ready to savage the mighty, or rummage through their bins. Elsewhere in Europe, government contracts and subsidies ensure that press barons will only defy the mighty so far.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[think]], [[believe]], or have an [[impression]] concerning.
##: {{ux|en|I '''feel''' that we need to try harder.}}
##: {{ux|en|We '''felt''' it (to be) our duty to call the police.}}
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 8|act=IV|scene=ii|passage=They promised me eternal happiness; And brought me garlands, Griffith, which I '''feel''' I am not worthy yet to wear.}}
##* {{RQ:Allingham China Governess|chapter=XIX|passage=When Timothy and Julia hurried up the staircase to the bedroom floor, where a considerable commotion was taking place, Tim took Barry Leach with him. He had him gripped firmly by the arm, since he '''felt''' it was not safe to let him loose, and he had no immediate idea what to do with him.}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive|copulative}} To experience an emotion or other mental state.
##: {{ux|en|He obviously '''feels''' strongly about it.}}
##: {{ux|en|She '''felt''' even more upset when she heard the details.}}
##: {{ux|en|I'm '''feel'''ing really nervous about my finals.}}
##* {{RQ:Churchill Celebrity|chapter=V|passage=Then we relapsed into a discomfited silence, and wished we were anywhere else. But Miss Thorn relieved the situation by laughing aloud, and with such a hearty enjoyment that instead of getting angry and more mortified we began to laugh ourselves, and instantly '''felt''' better.}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[sympathize]]; to have the sensibilities moved or affected.
##: {{ux|en|I '''feel''' for you and your plight.}}
##* {{RQ:Burke Revolution in France|passage=[She] '''feels''' with the dignity of a Roman matron.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1738|author=w:Alexander Pope|title=Epilogue to the Satires
|passage=who '''feel''' for all mankind}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To be or become [[aware]] of.
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To experience the [[consequence]]s of.
#: {{ux|en|'''Feel''' my wrath!}}
# {{lb|en|copulative}} To [[seem]] (through [[touch]] or otherwise).
#: {{ux|en|It looks like wood, but it '''feels''' more like plastic.}}
#: {{ux|en|It '''felt''' really strange to be back in my old kindergarten.}}
#: {{ux|en|This is supposed to be a party, but it '''feels''' more like a funeral!}}
# {{senseid|en|understand}} {{lb|en|transitive|US|slang}} To [[understand#Verb|understand]].
#: {{ux|en|I don't want you back here, ya '''feel''' me?}}
#* {{quote-song|en|title=Work It|artist=Missy Elliott|album=Under Construction|year=2002|passage=Shoot, errbody have the zipper jacket / And half of these thugs have the glove to match, ya '''feel''' me?}}

=====Usage notes=====
* When referring to the emotional state, most prescriptive grammarians prefer "I feel bad" to "I feel badly", but "I feel badly" is widely used this way in US English.
* In senses 2, 3, and 5, this is generally a [[stative verb]] that rarely takes the continuous inflection. See [[:Category:English stative verbs]]

